From 0294f3827887e12b4540a89979453d0a6daed745 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: heappi <heappi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 21:07:25 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] snapshot: the base does not keep its cells alive;
 tombstones

A chain of checkpoints pinned every cell that was alive at any
checkpoint until a full base was written again, so a program that
churns through temporaries grew without bound. The base now holds no
reference to its cells. When one is freed (free_gc_object(),
free_var_ref(), a promise resolution record, JS_FreeContext()),
snap_cell_freed() turns its slot into a tombstone; a stale map entry
whose address is reused is detected by comparing the pointer with the
slot. A delta or checkpoint lists the tombstones after its changed
cells, and a promotion marks them as recorded. The reader frees the
shells of dead cells before reading any body and rejects a reference to
one, a body for one, or a freed context.

As the base no longer pins cells, cycle collection is paused while a
snapshot is written and each cell is held while its body is serialized.

With 1,000 temporaries replaced at every checkpoint, live objects and
heap size stay flat over 30 checkpoints (1,187 objects, 184 KB) and the
restored runtime is equally small.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
